{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And Beth-El</b> — This is a sign that Jehu was from Beth-El, for it is the seat of his kingdom and his temple, for the meaning is doubled. And Yiphet erred, because Jehu was anointed in Ramoth-Gilead — and behold, Amaziah and Jehoram fought with Hazael at Ramoth-Gilead, and the Arameans struck Jehoram, who was on guard, he and all Israel, at Ramoth-Gilead. For thus it is written: Joram returned to Jezreel, the seat of his kingdom, to be healed, and left all the officers at Ramoth-Gilead, to stand guard, and with them was Jehu son of Nimshi. And when the young prophet anointed him there and the matter became known to Joram's officers who were standing guard, then they all conspired with Jehu; therefore the two kings could not stand before Jehu."], :book :amos, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The reading 'Amaziah and Jehoram fought' appears to conflate or corrupt names; the biblical account (2 Kings 8–9) names Jehoram (Joram) king of Israel and Ahaziah king of Judah as fighting Hazael at Ramoth-Gilead. Translated hyper-literally as found in the source."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Amos -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/amos"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:18:32.301184762Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}